# Rust units are named the way Rust writes them — `::` throughout, never the
# `#`/`.` Ruby uses. A Rust developer typing `Widget#run` is one contour has
# confused, and `Unit::id` is the single place that dialect lives.
#
# Also pinned here:
#   - module and impl nesting compose into one path
#   - a trait impl is named by the TYPE (`Widget::fmt`), because that is where
#     a reader looks for the body, not by the trait
#   - a bodyless trait signature is still a unit; it just has no hash
#   - the module a FILE declares is part of every unit's owner, which is why
#     `main` here is `app::main`. Rust derives module paths from file paths,
#     and layer 1 is forbidden one (DEC-003) — so the file layer composes it on
#     the way out instead (DEC-021). This line read `main` until M12b, and the
#     bare name is what made rq's five `tests::find` indistinguishable.
